  "worked_solution": "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3}k)^{2}+(-k)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3{k}^{2}+{k}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{k}^{2}} \\\\  & =2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\frac{-k}{\\sqrt{3}k})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})| \\\\  & =|-\\frac{\\pi }{6}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point lies in the fourth quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(2k,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).$",
